session 2026-08-25 (5) — GATE-alias: THE COLD ORACLE WAS MEASURING PAST THE END OF ITS OWN RULER, AND THE FIRST DIAGNOSIS WAS WRONG. The gate failed about one cold run in five with a whole file scored a few hundred milliseconds out of sync — reproduced on a clean `git archive HEAD` build, so not this session's code. The entry I wrote when I found it said the click↔flash PAIRING was ambiguous on a uniform 1 s grid and the estimator was picking the wrong alignment. That sounded right, it was consistent with every symptom, and it was not the cause. What settled it was refusing to keep arguing from the summary line: a failing run in a `--cold=N` matrix used to print its failure strings and throw the report away, so three sessions of cold flakes had been theorised about from one line of output. It now dumps the whole report to /tmp, and the first captured failure answered the question in a minute. THE NUMBERS: beep arrivals 1537, 2537, 3537, 4537, 5537 — a residual of 537 ms against the nominal k·1000 grid on EVERY one, while the flash side stalled 19-20. The AudioContext startup stall was 537 ms, perfectly regular and trivially measurable, and it was past TWO hard limits at once: estimateScheduleSkewFromArrivals refused any stall over MAX_SCHEDULE_STALL_MS = 450, so there was no correction at all and the gate fell to its RAW rung (which is why the failure line said `(raw)` and why "aliasing" was the wrong story); and flashSyncStats's pairing window is half the grid wide and centred on ZERO, so the +562 ms offset that stall produces is not representable and came back as its complement against the next flash, −438. The run was in sync. The ruler was too short and nothing said so. FOUR FIXES, each measured. (1) THE STALL IS ARITHMETIC, NOT A SEARCH: a beep is SCHEDULED at k·interval and can only render LATE — an AudioContext that has not finished starting cannot make a sound early — so arrival = k·interval + stall with stall in [0, interval) and k = floor(arrival/interval). The candidate-index sweep and its clamp are gone; a missed first beep still works, because that moves k and not the relation. The magnitude clamps inside isAliasedScheduleCorrection went with them, because what distinguishes an alias from a stall is not SIZE but whether the raw reading agrees — a real stall is visible in the raw offset too, and that clause is the one that caught every historical flake, so it stays. (2) THE PAIRING IS TOLD WHERE TO LOOK: the rig measures both reference schedules before it decodes anything and their difference is where a correctly-paired export must land, so the window is centred there instead of on zero; omitted, it is the old behaviour exactly. (3) THE PAIRING PICKS BY CONSENSUS, NOT BY PAIR COUNT — the old sweep kept whichever alignment produced the most pairs, which is injection-proven wrong: with a doubled detector it reports −400 ms where the truth is +50. Now every (onset, flash) pair proposes an offset and the one the most events independently agree on wins, ties to the smaller |offset|, and that prior is argued rather than convenient (the band is ±90 ms against a 1000 ms grid, so "a whole second out" needs more evidence than "in band", not less). (4) THE RIG WARMS ITS ENCODER BECAUSE PRODUCTION DOES — note 6's fourth instance. A Chrome PROCESS's first VideoEncoder pays a multi-second init and every oracle run is a fresh process; production pays it at MOUNT (prearm.ts → encoderWarm.ts) while this rig paid it DURING the take, which on a 6 s take is the whole take. Two dumped failures show it plainly: the render found all five flashes while the packet-copied exports found one and none. TWO VACUITY HOLES CLOSED WITH IT, and the second caught a real run within minutes of being written: the sync bands were guarded by `typeof mean === 'number'`, so a fast path that RAN but produced no number pushed no failure and the run passed green on a measurement never taken. Both the instant and the trimmed path now fail in that case. ONE SAF