session 2026-08-26 (5) — X6 FOLLOW-UP, TWO DEFECTS THE RIG COULD NOT HAVE FOUND: a frame-driven raw channel must keep its own cadence. Both were found by driving the DEPLOYED build, after every rig gate was green, and both have one cause. MediaStreamTrackProcessor is FRAME-DRIVEN — a screen that is not changing delivers no frames at all — and MediaRecorder never had this problem because it encodes at its own cadence whatever the source does. (1) A 9 s take through the X6 path recorded "2 frames encoded of 2 in" on both raw channels. The live composite in the SAME take logged 7 frames of which 6 were keep-alive: it has carried a keep-alive since O4 for exactly this reason, and the raw path shipped without one. Fixed by keeping a clone of the most recent picture and re-encoding it when nothing has reached the encoder for a second. Two constructions came with it, both copied from compositor.worker.ts because it solved this first: the channel's timeline is now built from ARRIVAL stamps rather than source timestamps (startOffsetMs is already an arrival quantity, so the file's t=0 and its placement on the session timeline come from one clock — and a keep-alive frame has no source timestamp to use anyway), and the worker min-filters its own clock offset out of the arrival stamps the frame messages already carry, because the keep-alive timer fires in the worker while the main thread owns the clock. (2) EVEN WITH THE KEEP-ALIVE the channel declared durationMs 0 and the editor opened the take as AUDIO-ONLY with a waveform, which is how it was spotted. A channel's LENGTH is how long it was LIVE, not how long frames happened to arrive: with a static source exactly one frame arrives, so first === last. The stop message now carries the MAIN thread's stop instant, the worker emits one final keep-alive at it, and the duration is that span — which also means the file COVERS the length it declares rather than falling short of it. Tail gap on the rig went from -344 ms to 9 ms, i.e. th