session 2026-08-29 (1) — THE WALL-CLOCK HOLD IS SYMMETRIC NOW, ON ROBERT'S GO. Robert: "in long video more than hour on preview player tab sound get a little slower than screen video overtime (about second after one hour) / on export seems same". The hold only ever PADDED and its own comment said so ("the wall only ever moves it FORWARD"), which is half a guard: video is wall-stamped, audio is sample-counted at the context's NOMINAL rate, so an audio clock slower than the wall was repaid and a FASTER one was invisible and unbounded. Driven over an hour of quanta through the shipped class: -278 ppm padded 960 ms and ended 41 ms early (fine), +278 ppm padded nothing and ended +1001 ms LATE — Robert's second, to the millisecond. 278 ppm is an ordinary crystal difference between an audio device and the system clock. Nothing in the app could see it, and that is structural, not an oversight: the file's timestamps stay self-consistent and merely stretched, so the preview's per-element slew reads zero drift while the CONTENT sits a second behind. The 2026-08-25 "DRIFT IS DEAD" verdict (beta-1 = -0.003 ms/s) was a 120 s SYNTHETIC cell — no external device, hence no device clock to mismatch, hence nothing to find.
A TRIM IS NOT A PAD MIRRORED, and that is the whole design. A pad fills time nothing was rendered into, so silence is honest. A trim removes time that HOLDS AUDIO, so the naive mirror — splice out a chunk once the deficit passes the threshold — would delete speech and click at both ends, worse than the drift it fixes. Two things instead: (a) the correction is RATE-LIMITED to 0.2 % of any one batch, a hard per-batch bound and not an average with a fractional loan, so the timeline is walked back over tens of seconds and never yanked — 3.5 cents of pitch, ~7x the error it must track; (b) the frames are removed by RESAMPLING the batch shorter (compressInterleaved / compressPlanar), so there is no splice at all, nothing to click and nothing to fade. Dead band stays symmetric at 80 ms, inside the 90 ms this build certifies, so ordinary jitter still corrects nothing and a healthy take is bit-identical. Nine existing tests unchanged and green; the min-over-settle-window estimator needed no change because arrival error is one-sided, so min strips jitter in BOTH directions. Oracle PASS (sync 43.9 ms, instant + smartcut + render all engaged). The take now carries its own verdict: ChannelDiagnostics.trimmedMs, beside paddedMs, and a console line naming the measured ppm.

