than the 3024x1964 screen (814 MB at 2.25 Mbps of 8 requested) — is the screen encoder starved, or does
still content legitimately compress to 28 % of its request? Measured with the o11 bits audit, one
machine, one 8 Mbps request, two contents (`scripts/gate.sh node scripts/exp.mjs o11 '{"takeMs":10000}'`
— note the TASKS line said `npm run exp -- bits` and there IS no `bits` experiment; the id is `o11`).
CAPTURE encoder (MediaRecorder, the raw channel's own path), 8 Mbps requested: screen-like still content
(an editor page that scrolls one line every 2.5 s) achieved 2.53 Mbps = 31.6 % of the request; the
motion source (a full-frame gradient that changes everywhere every frame) achieved 4.43 Mbps = 55.4 %.
The field's 28 % sits inside the still band. RENDER encoder, same 8 Mbps request: motion held
2.400-2.409 Mbps — 30.0-30.1 % — across five independent GOP renders, screen ran 1.25-2.63 Mbps
(15.6-32.8 %) as the cadence changed.
THREE THINGS SETTLE IT. (1) NEITHER content reaches its request. The most-changing picture this rig can
paint stops at 55 %, so "achieved below requested" is not a starvation signal at all — `videoBitsPerSecond`
is a target the encoder is free to undershoot, and it undershoots by content. (2) A starved encoder does
not hold a rate constant to 0.1 % across five separate renders; the motion lane did. (3) PSNR against the
2 s baseline read 66.1-93.0 dB, far above the ~45 dB at which two files are the same picture — no quality
was given up to reach those bytes. And the camera outwriting the screen is the same fact from the other
side: bytes track CHANGE, not pixel count, so a moving 720p face costs more than a still 3024x1964 page.
The 48-minute field take held 2.25 Mbps to the end, which is itself against starvation — a starved
encoder degrades as it goes.
WHAT THIS DOES NOT PROVE, stated so nobody over-reads it: the rig paints 1920x1080 and its camera is
synthetic, so it establishes the CONTENT mechanism and does not by itself rule out something specific to
3024x1964. Anyone who still suspects starvation there should read the FRAME accounting of that take, not
its byte count — bytes cannot distinguish the two and frames can. No product change follows from this;
the size lane's lever is content-side (X17's census, SIZE-CODEC), not a bitrate the encoder is declining
to spend.

