session 2026-09-04 — "GO O4": STEP 1 ANSWERED. THE TRANSFERRED CAPTURE FRAME IS NOT READ BACK, AND THE
35.6 ms THIS TASK QUOTES WAS THE RIG MEASURING ITS OWN SYNTHETIC SOURCE.
THE QUESTION, open since 2026-08-24: are transferred capture frames GPU-resident in the compositor
worker, or is every `texImage2D` a readback? It gated the backend by the task's own words.
THE ANSWER, and it needs no calibration because it is an inequality: a readback followed by an upload
can never be CHEAPER than uploading the same bytes, in the same format, at the same size, straight
from CPU memory — that is the same upload with a readback added in front. Production's exact shape
(real getDisplayMedia → MediaStreamTrackProcessor on the main thread → the VideoFrame TRANSFERRED to a
worker → texImage2D into WebGL2, one sync per 24 uploads so the sync is amortised), against a control
that is those same bytes from a JS ArrayBuffer: 3024x1964 NV12, 8.9 MB — upload 0.72-0.80 ms against
the CPU control's 3.23-3.41 ms = 0.22-0.23x. At the shipped 1080p rung, 1662x1078 NV12, 2.7 MB —
0.27-0.49 ms against 1.29-1.35 ms = 0.20-0.38x. FIVE runs, two screen sizes. NOT A READBACK.
WHAT IT IS INSTEAD: on Apple Silicon the frame is an IOSurface in unified memory, so the binary the
task posed does not exist — the same frame is GPU-texturable at 0.20-0.38x a CPU upload AND
CPU-mappable at 0.82-1.79x a CPU-resident frame's memcpy (`copyTo`). Both, at once.
THE 4K NUMBER, which every O4 gate is written against. Paint = upload + a full-size draw, amortised
and GPU-synced: the real screen path costs 0.29-0.31 ms/Mpx, so 4K (8.29 Mpx) projects to 2.4-2.6 ms
per frame — 7-8 % of the 33 ms budget at 30 fps, 15 % at 60. The quoted 35.6 ms is wrong by ~14x for
screen capture. WHERE IT CAME FROM: o4step2 fed an uncapped 4K CANVAS captureStream. Measured here,
that source is BGRA at 33.2 MB/frame and costs 2.03 ms/Mpx — 16.8-17.1 ms per 4K frame, 6.6x the real
screen path per pixel and the same order as 35.6. The rig was measuring itself (note 10, fifth time).
CAPTURERS DIFFER AND THE SYNTHETIC ONE IS THE OUTLIER — this is the reusable part: real screen NV12
0.29-0.31 ms/Mpx and upload 0.20-0.23x a CPU upload · real TAB capture I420 0.68 ms/Mpx and upload
0.95x, i.e. tab capture is NOT GPU-cheap and is a different pixel path from screen capture · canvas
captureStream BGRA 0.89-0.95 ms/Mpx at 1080p and 2.03 at 4K, upload 0.96-3.36x. Every composite number
this repo has taken through `?synthetic=1` was taken on the most expensive of the three.
WHAT WEBGPU CAN STILL RECLAIM, stated so it is never overstated again: `importExternalTexture` removes
the upload, and the upload is 40-50 % of the paint — 0.27 ms at the 1080p rung, 0.73 ms at 3024x1964,
~1.1 ms projected at 4K, per frame. Against a 16.7 ms budget at 60 fps that is 1.6-6.6 %; against the
export loop, where draw is already measured at 1.95 %, under 1 %. Robert's ruling stands that a 2 %
lever is a lever (robert (20)); the size of this one is now measured rather than assumed, and the
"whole compositor throughput gap" alternative is dead — the 2026-08-24 paradox (169 fps in the encoder
yet 101 frames finding six outstanding) is NOT a readback and stays unexplained; the encoder remains
the measured wall.
TWO DEFECTS FOUND IN THE INSTRUMENTS, both fixed here:
(1) `gl.finish()` DOES NOT WAIT FOR THE GPU in a Chrome worker. A fragment shader doing 64 texture
fetches per pixel over 5.9 Mpx cost the same 0.03 ms as one doing 16 — 12 tera-fetches/s, i.e. finish()
returned early and every number it "fenced" was an enqueue cost. Reading one pixel back cannot return
early. `compositorGL.finish()` documented itself as blocking and did not; it now reads a pixel. It is
measurement-only and inert on the shipped path (PROBE_GPU = false), so no behaviour moved — but
`?probegpu`'s `gpuMs` and any future GPU reading would have been a lie.
(2) A NODE-SPAWNED CHROME CANNOT CAPTURE A REAL SCREEN ON THIS MAC, and that is why no rig here ever
did. macOS attributes a TCC permission to the RESPONSIBLE process: Chrome spawned from node inherits
node's, node has no Screen Recording grant, and getDisplayMedia fails instantly with
`NotAllowedError: Permission denied by system` — the SAME probe, same flags, same profile, launched
with `open -na` (launchd responsible, so the grant is Chrome's own) answers OK. `launchChrome({viaOpen})`
in scripts/lib/chrome.mjs, opt-in, spawn still the default. Every screen-capture rig in this repo can
now measure a real display instead of settling for a tab.
GATES: 1,182 unit tests + typecheck green · the rig carries its own lever (heavy64/heavy16 must exceed
1.5x or it prints that its numbers are void; measured 1.76-1.77x) · four runs on two screen sizes ·
evidence docs/qa/o4-gpu-residency.json · NOTHING IN THE PRODUCT CHANGED except compositorGL's
measurement-only fence.
OPEN AND ROBERT'S: whether the backend is still worth building at 0.27-1.1 ms/frame.

