WHAT IT FIXES, and it was measured before it was built: `MediaStreamTrackProcessor` does not wait for
a starved reader, it DROPS (B12). B12 widened the platform's ~87 ms buffer to 4 s; this removes the
deadline instead, because a reader on its own thread is never the thing that is late. Through the
product, 45 s takes with the buffer cut to 100 ms so only the thread can save it and the main thread
blocked 32 s: **the page reader lost 26.7 s of real audio and the wall-clock hold repaid it as
silence; the worker reader lost 1.56 s against its own 1.53 s control.** The premise was PROBED
first (`scripts/x11a-workertap.mjs`) because a transferred ReadableStream could have been a proxy the
original thread still cranks: 20.0 s of block cost the page reader 13.8-15.3 s of a 21 s window and
the worker reader nothing, worst step 3 ms.
THE TRANSFER IS THE CAPABILITY GATE — attempted in a try/catch, falling through to A1's main-thread
pump, so a platform that refuses (Safari today) records exactly as before. No capability table.
WHAT BLOCKED IT FOR A DAY, AND HOW IT WAS ANSWERED. The anchor dates sample 0 from when a batch
ARRIVES, and a min-filter strips jitter but never a constant, so a reader one postMessage away would
place the whole take late — read at +14 ms (anchor 88.3 -> 102.1 ms) on the first build. THE METHOD
WAS THE PROBLEM, not the reader. Differencing two anchors cannot resolve 14 ms on this machine, and
its own control says so: `x11a-anchor.mjs --arms=main,main` runs the SAME thread against itself and
reads a paired **-50.3 ms on the mic and -45.3 ms on system audio** (pairs -27.8, -72.8) between
cells that differ in nothing. The first take in a browser process anchors 85.6+-5.2 ms and the second
38.3 ms, so an A/B that did not alternate its order would report that position effect as an arm
difference — which is the likeliest origin of the original +14 ms. Across 24 readings on one build on
an idle machine, `rawAnchorMs` spans **19 to 350 ms**.
SO THE MECHANISM IS MEASURED DIRECTLY. The batch carries the moment the WORKER completed it
(`performance.timeOrigin + performance.now()`, converted with the page's own origin) and the take now
records what that correction is worth — `tapHandoffMs` / `tapHandoffP95Ms`, main-thread receipt minus
worker stamp: **0.05 ms median of six cells, worst p95 1 ms, both channels, every cell inside
-0.1..0.2 ms.** The direction is structural: the stamp precedes its own delivery, so the correction
can only place a take EARLIER, never later, and by at most a millisecond.
AND THE PRODUCT'S OWN SYNC BAND AGREES, on ONE build, 7 cold oracle cells per arm
(`--query=audiotapthread=main` is the control arm): **sync p50 worker 49.7+-12.0 ms against main
